{
  "requirement": "The enabled Habeas Symposium plugin MUST request a global Cargo installation of the `habeas` CLI and use that installation as its session-start hook command, so the CLI is available when the hook advises an agent to run `habeas setup`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The requirement states two structural obligations. Both are implemented by the Symposium metadata in `Cargo.toml`, and I found no gap.\n\n1. Clause: \"MUST request a global Cargo installation of the `habeas` CLI\". An alternative such as `source = \"npm\"`, a different `crate`, or `global = false` would behave differently and violate it. The `[[package.metadata.symposium.installations]]` entry in `Cargo.toml` supplies each part:\n   - `table[package.metadata.symposium.installations]/pair[source]` is `source = \"cargo\"`.\n   - `table[package.metadata.symposium.installations]/pair[crate]` is `crate = \"habeas\"`.\n   - `table[package.metadata.symposium.installations]/pair[global]` is `global = true`.\n   - `table[package.metadata.symposium.installations]/pair[name]` names the installation `habeas`.\n\n2. Clause: \"use that installation as its session-start hook command\". An alternative with a hook `command` that doesn't refer to the installation, such as an absolute path or another tool, would violate it. The `[[package.metadata.symposium.hooks]]` entry in `Cargo.toml` supplies this:\n   - `table[package.metadata.symposium.hooks]/pair[command]` is `command = \"habeas\"`, which matches the installation's `name`.\n   - `table[package.metadata.symposium.hooks]/pair[name]` identifies the hook that carries this command. The hook's `event` is `SessionStart`, which `requirements.integration.symposium_setup_hook` covers.\n\nThe installation's `executable = \"habeas\"` pair has no marker. The text doesn't name an executable. The installation's name and crate already satisfy the requirement, so the executable pair is discretionary here.\n\nThe remaining wording is behavioral: the CLI is available when the hook advises an agent to run `habeas setup`. No other implementation root is needed. The hook's advice text is a separate requirement.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement has two behavioral obligations. The first is that the plugin requests a global Cargo installation of the `habeas` CLI. The second is that the session-start hook uses that installation as its command.\n\n`tests/repository.rs#fn[symposium_plugin_installs_the_cli_globally_for_its_session_start_hook]` covers both. It parses `Cargo.toml` and checks the following:\n- There is exactly one installation, with `source` = \"cargo\", `crate` = \"habeas\", `executable` = \"habeas\" and `global` = true. This covers the global Cargo installation request.\n- There is exactly one hook, with `event` = \"SessionStart\". Its `command` equals the installation's `executable` value, so the hook runs the installed CLI. Its `args` are [\"hook\", \"session-start\"].\n\nThe requirement is satisfied by declarative manifest metadata, so asserting on the parsed manifest checks the behavior the text specifies. I found no separate \"enabled\" switch in the plugin metadata that would call for another scenario. The \"so the CLI is available\" clause states a purpose, not a separate obligation. The advice to run `habeas setup` belongs to `requirements.integration.symposium_setup_hook`, not to this requirement. A weakened configuration would fail the assertions. Examples are a non-global installation, a different crate or source, or a hook command that differs from the installed executable.\n\nThe test is marked with {test} for the subject.",
  "references": "a6c4f0e64a68bbc6401b55bf90de985d33452cf5264c17d707356e164d24e9dc",
