        "status": "success",
        "rationale": "The requirement states two structural obligations. Both are implemented by the Symposium metadata in `Cargo.toml`, and I found no gap.\n\n1. Clause: \"MUST request a global Cargo installation of the `habeas` CLI\". An alternative such as `source = \"npm\"`, a different `crate`, or `global = false` would behave differently and violate it. The `[[package.metadata.symposium.installations]]` entry in `Cargo.toml` supplies each part:\n   - `table[package.metadata.symposium.installations]/pair[source]` is `source = \"cargo\"`.\n   - `table[package.metadata.symposium.installations]/pair[crate]` is `crate = \"habeas\"`.\n   - `table[package.metadata.symposium.installations]/pair[global]` is `global = true`.\n   - `table[package.metadata.symposium.installations]/pair[name]` names the installation `habeas`.\n\n2. Clause: \"use that installation as its session-start hook command\". An alternative with a hook `command` that doesn't refer to the installation, such as an absolute path or another tool, would violate it. The `[[package.metadata.symposium.hooks]]` entry in `Cargo.toml` supplies this:\n   - `table[package.metadata.symposium.hooks]/pair[command]` is `command = \"habeas\"`, which matches the installation's `name`.\n   - `table[package.metadata.symposium.hooks]/pair[name]` identifies the hook that carries this command. The hook's `event` is `SessionStart`, which `requirements.integration.symposium_setup_hook` covers.\n\nThe installation's `executable = \"habeas\"` pair has no marker. The text doesn't name an executable. The installation's name and crate already satisfy the requirement, so the executable pair is discretionary here.\n\nThe remaining wording is behavioral: the CLI is available when the hook advises an agent to run `habeas setup`. No other implementation root is needed. The hook's advice text is a separate requirement."
