      "output": {
        "status": "success",
        "rationale": "The requirement has two behavioral obligations. The first is that the plugin requests a global Cargo installation of the `habeas` CLI. The second is that the session-start hook uses that installation as its command.\n\n`tests/repository.rs#fn[symposium_plugin_installs_the_cli_globally_for_its_session_start_hook]` covers both. It parses `Cargo.toml` and checks the following:\n- There is exactly one installation, with `source` = \"cargo\", `crate` = \"habeas\", `executable` = \"habeas\" and `global` = true. This covers the global Cargo installation request.\n- There is exactly one hook, with `event` = \"SessionStart\". Its `command` equals the installation's `executable` value, so the hook runs the installed CLI. Its `args` are [\"hook\", \"session-start\"].\n\nThe requirement is satisfied by declarative manifest metadata, so asserting on the parsed manifest checks the behavior the text specifies. I found no separate \"enabled\" switch in the plugin metadata that would call for another scenario. The \"so the CLI is available\" clause states a purpose, not a separate obligation. The advice to run `habeas setup` belongs to `requirements.integration.symposium_setup_hook`, not to this requirement. A weakened configuration would fail the assertions. Examples are a non-global installation, a different crate or source, or a hook command that differs from the installed executable.\n\nThe test is marked with {test} for the subject."
      }
    }
